00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.190
So why is API
operations important?

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:20.060
Well, APIs are packages
of data and functionality

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:23.060
that developers use to build
new digital experiences quickly

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:24.110
and easily.

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:26.520
Since they encompass business
critical information,

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.550
it's extremely important
for operations teams

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:30.980
to ensure the APIs are always
available and performing

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:32.390
as expected.

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:34.640
But as API programs
scale, it becomes

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.120
practically impossible to
manage individual APIs.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.400
To reduce manual
overhead and operators,

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.280
Apigee brings the power of
Google's industry-leading AI

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:45.560
and machine learning
to your APIs.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:48.920
A great example of such a use
case is anomaly detection.

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:51.500
Anomaly detection
applies AI-powered rules

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:54.567
that trigger alerts based on a
set of predefined conditions.

00:00:54.567 --> 00:00:56.150
This eliminates the
need for operators

00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:59.660
to manually adjust the
monitoring thresholds on APIs.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:02.060
As API traffic
changes by season,

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:05.850
auto-adjusted thresholds reduce
a lot of false alert noise.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:07.640
Therefore, operators
can now focus

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:10.110
on things that really
matter to their business.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.920
Let's take a closer
look at this feature.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.830
You can view all anomaly
events under the API Monitoring

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:17.430
menu of Apigee.

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:21.410
It shows a complete list of
anomalies identified by Apigee.

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:23.240
Using the power of
machine learning,

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.540
these events are auto-generated
based on historical API data.

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:27.980
This eliminates
the need for users

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:30.560
to manually set up alerts.

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:32.420
The actionable link on
each of these events

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:35.690
will take you to an Investigate
view of the anomaly.

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:37.640
Here, you can see that
the error rate surged

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:39.440
at around 4 o'clock,
and the system

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:42.020
was able to catch the anomaly.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:44.570
Below the graph, you can see
the fault code, which shows

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:47.510
the root cause of this anomaly.

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:49.130
You can further
investigate this event

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:51.320
by adding more layers,
such as API Proxy.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.230
This shows the details of the
proxy for which this event was

00:01:57.230 --> 00:01:59.270
triggered.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:01.640
You can also use this
analysis to identify

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.260
the source of the error.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:05.930
In this case, you can see that
the fault source of the proxy

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:08.690
is the Apigee platform.

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:10.860
In addition to the
system-generated events,

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.370
you can also set up alerts to
get real-time notifications

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:16.970
when anomaly events
are triggered.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.340
The alert can be set up by
creating an alert policy

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:21.005
in Google Cloud's
monitoring service.

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.340
Once an alert is triggered,
you'll get a notification.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.120
Here is a snapshot
of an email alert.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:31.220
It provides complete
details of the anomaly,

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:36.500
such as Timestamp, Project,
Policy, and Threshold buttons.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.300
Users can click on the
View Incident button

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:42.200
to quickly investigate and find
the root cause of the error.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.120
In summary, Apigee
brings the power

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:46.310
of Google's industry-leading
AI with features

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:49.340
like anomaly detection to
simplify API operations

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:52.040
so that you can deliver seamless
and connected experiences

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.313
for your customers.

00:02:53.313 --> 00:02:54.980
Now it's your turn
to try it out and get

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:57.470
started by following the links
in the description below.

